/* Domain 45 — Technical Project Management & Leadership: running the job, from fiber builds to software (facts as of October 2026) */
BOOTCAMP.phases.push({
id:"p45", num:45, title:"Technical Project Management & Leadership", short:"PM",
tagline:"From the bore rig to the trailer: scope it, schedule it, cost it, de-risk it and lead the crew through it — on fiber builds and software projects alike — and earn CAPM or Project+ on the way.",
prereq:"Domains 20 (Scrum, Kanban and estimation inside a software team), 24 (fiber outside plant, the as-built and field safety), 26 (IT operations, ITIL and the career ladder) and 33 (governance and risk by the numbers). Domain 46's motivation science feeds Module 6. A spreadsheet is the only tool you need to start.",
outcomes:[
"Tell projects from operations, programs and portfolios; choose a predictive, adaptive or hybrid life cycle; and quote the major failure studies with their sources and limits.",
"Initiate and plan a job: business case, charter, stakeholder analysis on a power-interest grid, requirements, a WBS, three-point and reference-class estimates, a critical-path schedule and a budget with reserves.",
"Control execution with earned value (PV, EV, AC, CPI, SPI, EAC, TCPI), change control, quality control, the right contract type and one-page status reports fed by daily as-builts.",
"Manage risk by the numbers — risk registers, probability–impact scoring, EMV, decision trees, Monte Carlo percentiles, reserves — and treat field safety (job hazard analysis) and cyber risk as the same discipline.",
"Run Scrum and Kanban precisely, forecast with velocity ranges and Little's law, judge SAFe and LeSS honestly, and design hybrid plans that join a construction schedule to software sprints.",
"Lead people: situational leadership, motivation, meetings, one-on-ones, SBI feedback, conflict modes, principled negotiation with a BATNA, managing up, psychological safety and PMI's Code of Ethics.",
"Use Jira, Microsoft Project, Smartsheet and ProjectLibre sensibly; build a dashboard; explain programs, portfolios and PMOs; and pass PMI CAPM or CompTIA Project+ (PK0-005) with a 10-week plan.",
"Map a realistic path from technician to crew lead to project manager, with verified 2025 pay data and the PMP and PSM I as next steps."
],
texts:["PMBOK Guide — Eighth Edition and The Standard for Project Management (PMI, November 2025)","The Scrum Guide — Ken Schwaber & Jeff Sutherland (November 2020, still the official version)","Making Things Happen: Mastering Project Management — Scott Berkun (O'Reilly, revised edition, 2008)","How Big Things Get Done — Bent Flyvbjerg & Dan Gardner (Currency, 2023)"],
modules:[

{id:"p45m1", title:"What a Project Is and Why Projects Fail", minutes:50,
outcomes:["Distinguish projects from operations, programs and portfolios with field and IT examples.","Explain the triple constraint, competing constraints and why value (output → outcome → benefit) is the real target.","Choose a predictive, adaptive, incremental or hybrid life cycle and justify the choice.","Quote the major failure studies with their sources, numbers and limits.","Name the roles on a fiber build and a software project, write a RACI row, and describe the PMI Talent Triangle."],
body:`<p>Why do most projects miss their targets, and what does the person running them actually do about it? You already know half the answer from the ground: the bore that hit rock, the locate ticket that was not ready, the reel that arrived short. This domain gives you the other half, the view from the trailer, where those surprises are predicted, priced, scheduled around and explained to the customer before they happen. Learn it and you stop being the technician things happen to and become the one who runs the job.</p>
<h4>Projects, operations and the constraints</h4>
<p>PMI defines a <strong>project</strong> as a temporary endeavor undertaken to create a unique product, service or result. Temporary means it has a start and an end; unique means it has never been done exactly this way before. Building fiber to 412 homes in a new subdivision is a project. Keeping that network running, answering trouble tickets and fixing cuts is <strong>operations</strong>: ongoing, repetitive work measured against service levels forever (Domain 26). Your crew bores every week, but each job has its own route, permits, soil and customer, so each is a project; the repeatable parts become templates and checklists that make the next one cheaper.</p>
<p>Every project trades among constraints. The <strong>triple constraint</strong> (scope, schedule and cost, with quality in the middle) says you cannot move one corner without moving another: add 60 homes and either the date slips or you add a crew. Modern guidance adds resources and risk as competing constraints and insists the triangle is not the goal. The goal is <em>value</em>. The PMBOK Guide — Eighth Edition (PMI, November 2025) lists "focus on value" among its six principles, and the chain to carry in your head is output → outcome → benefit: 412 homes passed is an output, 165 subscribers is an outcome, and the revenue and the families online are the benefit. A job delivered on time and on budget that nobody uses failed at the only thing that justified it.</p>
<h4>Life cycles: predictive, adaptive, hybrid</h4>
<p>A <strong>predictive</strong> (plan-driven) life cycle fixes scope early and plans the whole job in detail. It is right when requirements are stable and change is expensive: you cannot iterate a directional bore under a highway, because the permit fixes the route. An <strong>adaptive</strong> (agile) life cycle plans in short iterations and expects requirements to emerge; it is right for software, where a working increment every two weeks beats a specification nobody can predict. <strong>Incremental</strong> delivery hands over usable pieces: light the subdivision one serving area at a time so revenue starts before the last splice. A <strong>hybrid</strong> life cycle runs both at once: the plant is built predictively while the portal that activates those homes is built in sprints, synchronized at milestones. Choosing it is the first planning decision; Module 5 shows when each wins.</p>
<h4>How often projects fail, with sources</h4>
<table><tr><th>Study</th><th>Sample</th><th>Finding</th></tr>
<tr><td>Standish Group CHAOS 2020</td><td>Software projects</td><td>31% successful, 50% challenged, 19% failed</td></tr>
<tr><td>McKinsey and Oxford, 2012</td><td>5,400+ IT projects</td><td>Projects over $15M ran 45% over budget and 7% over time and delivered 56% less value than predicted; 17% went badly enough to threaten the company</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flyvbjerg and Budzier, 2011</td><td>1,471 IT projects</td><td>Average cost overrun 27%, but one in six was a black swan averaging 200% over cost and almost 70% over schedule</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flyvbjerg and Gardner, 2023</td><td>16,000+ projects of all kinds</td><td>Under half on budget; 8.5% on budget and on time; 0.5% on budget, on time and delivering the promised benefits</td></tr></table>
<p>Read them critically. Standish's definitions and sampling have been criticized (Eveleens and Verhoef, <em>IEEE Software</em>, 2010), and "challenged" lumps a two-week slip together with a disaster. Yet every dataset agrees on two lessons. Failure is the base rate, not the exception. And the distribution is fat-tailed: the average overrun hides the few jobs that blow up by multiples. Flyvbjerg's diagnosis is that most damage is done at the start, by optimism and by pressure to make the numbers look good, so his prescription is "think slow, act fast": plan and test cheaply until the plan is proven, then execute quickly.</p>
<h4>Roles, and the PM job in telecom and IT</h4>
<p>The <strong>sponsor</strong> funds the project, owns the business case and makes the calls above your authority. The project manager is accountable for delivering the objectives; the team does the work; functional managers own the people; a <strong>PMO</strong> sets standards (Module 7). Every <strong>stakeholder</strong>, anyone who affects or is affected by the work, has to be identified and managed. A <strong>RACI</strong> chart assigns each deliverable who is Responsible, Accountable (exactly one), Consulted and Informed. On a fiber build the cast is concrete: the ISP or utility that owns the network, the prime contractor's PM, a construction manager or superintendent, foremen and crew leads, the OSP engineer who drew the prints, a permit coordinator, 811 locators, the owner's inspector, and splicing and testing leads. A telecom-construction PM's week is look-ahead schedules, production against plan from the daily as-builts, permit and make-ready chasing, change orders, unit-price invoicing, safety tailgates and customer calls. An IT PM's week has the same skeleton: backlog and release plans, dependencies, vendor contracts, risks and status. In Scrum (Domain 20) the classic PM duties are spread across the Product Owner and Scrum Master accountabilities.</p>
<h4>The PMI Talent Triangle</h4>
<p>PMI's Talent Triangle, introduced in 2015 and renamed in 2022, names the three skill sets employers hire for and that PMI uses to categorize continuing-education credits: <strong>Ways of Working</strong> (predictive, agile and hybrid technique: scheduling, estimating, earned value, Scrum), <strong>Power Skills</strong> (communication, collaboration, leadership, strategic thinking: Module 6) and <strong>Business Acumen</strong> (strategy, finance and the industry: why this build matters to the ISP). Technicians who move up arrive strong on the field side of ways of working and thin on the other two. This domain builds all three on purpose.</p>
<h4>Exam mapping</h4>
<p>On the CAPM this module is Domain 1, Project Management Fundamentals and Core Concepts (36%), tasks 1 and 3 (life cycles and processes; roles and responsibilities), plus the first task of Domain 2 (when a predictive approach fits) and of Domain 3 (when an adaptive one fits). On CompTIA Project+ PK0-005 it is Domain 1.0, project management concepts (33%): project versus operations, life cycles, roles, and the triple constraint.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Same subdivision, two scorecards.</strong> The ISP's business case for Oak Hollow: 412 homes passed at about $1,200 each ($494,400), a 40% take rate (165 subscribers) at $70 a month, so $138,600 a year and a simple payback of 494,400 ÷ 138,600 ≈ 3.6 years. The build finished three weeks late and 6% over budget ($524,064), so on the CHAOS scorecard it is "challenged." But a door-to-door sign-up campaign run during construction lifted the take rate to 46%: 190 subscribers × $840 a year = $159,600, and payback became 524,064 ÷ 159,600 ≈ 3.3 years, faster than planned. A competitor's on-time, on-budget build next door sits at a 22% take rate. Judged by the triangle, the competitor won; judged by benefits, Oak Hollow did. A PM who understands value reports both, and protects the activity (the sign-up campaign) that drives the benefit even when it costs a few days.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"On time and on budget means the project succeeded.\" Only if it delivers the benefit that justified it; in Flyvbjerg's 16,000-project data just 0.5% hit cost, time and benefits, and benefits are the hardest of the three.","\"Agile means no planning.\" Agile plans constantly over short horizons: the Product Goal, Sprint Planning and release forecasts are all plans, revisited with evidence.","\"The PM is the boss of everyone on the job.\" PMs often have little formal authority (a subcontractor's crew answers to its own foreman); they lead through the plan, information and trust, which is Module 6."],
problems:[
{q:"Classify each as project or operations and justify: (a) splicing a new 288-count feeder for a subdivision, (b) quarterly OTDR audits of a live network, (c) migrating the help desk to a new ticketing system, (d) answering fiber-cut trouble tickets.", a:"(a) Project: temporary, unique deliverable. (b) Operations: recurring and repetitive. (c) Project: one-time change with an end. (d) Operations: ongoing service work measured against SLAs."},
{q:"The sponsor wants to drop 60 homes to save three weeks. At a 40% take rate and $70 a month, what annual revenue is at stake, and what two questions do you ask before agreeing?", a:"60 × 0.40 = 24 subscribers × $840 = $20,160 a year. Ask what the three weeks are worth (penalties, a competitor's launch date, revenue starting earlier) and what it would cost to come back later for the 60 homes (remobilization, a second permit)."},
{q:"Pick a life cycle for each and justify it: (a) a bore under a state highway with a permit-fixed route; (b) a dispatch app for field crews whose requirements are unclear; (c) a citywide FTTH launch with plant plus a customer portal.", a:"(a) Predictive: scope fixed by the permit, change is expensive. (b) Adaptive: requirements emerge, feedback is cheap. (c) Hybrid: predictive plant, agile portal, synchronized at milestones such as first area lit."},
{q:"A project reports 'on budget' every month, yet at 80% of the schedule only 50% of the footage is installed. Diagnose the reporting failure and name the measure that would have exposed it early.", a:"Spending is being compared with the budget, not with the work accomplished. Earned value (EV versus PV and AC, Module 3) would have shown an SPI near 0.6 and the cost overrun coming months earlier."},
{q:"Write the RACI row for 'submit the right-of-way permit application' with the PM, permit coordinator, OSP engineer and sponsor.", a:"Permit coordinator R (does it), PM A (answers for it, exactly one A), OSP engineer C (supplies and checks the prints), sponsor I (told when it is filed and approved)."}
],
checks:[{q:"PMI's definition of a project?",a:"A temporary endeavor undertaken to create a unique product, service or result."},{q:"The three sides of the PMI Talent Triangle?",a:"Ways of Working, Power Skills, Business Acumen."},{q:"In Flyvbjerg and Gardner's data, what share of projects hit budget, schedule and benefits?",a:"About 0.5% of 16,000+ projects."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA why a project that is late and over budget can still be the better project, using output, outcome and benefit, then have ADA challenge you with the CHAOS, McKinsey and Flyvbjerg numbers and their limits."},

{id:"p45m2", title:"Initiating and Planning: Charter, Stakeholders, Scope, Estimates and the Critical Path", minutes:60,
outcomes:["Write a business-case summary and a one-page charter with measurable objectives and named authority.","Analyze stakeholders on a power-interest grid and plan their engagement.","Build a WBS that obeys the 100% rule, for a fiber build and for a software project.","Estimate with parametric, three-point (PERT) and reference-class methods, and with story points.","Compute a critical path with forward and backward passes, read float, choose between crashing and fast-tracking, and set baselines and reserves."],
body:`<p>How do you turn "connect 412 homes in Oak Hollow by March" into a plan that tells every crew what to do on a Tuesday? Planning is the part of the job a technician never sees, the part that decides most outcomes (Module 1), and the most testable part of both exams.</p>
<h4>Business case and charter</h4>
<p>The <strong>business case</strong> says why the work is worth doing: the problem or opportunity, the options considered, costs, benefits and risks. The ISP's case for Oak Hollow is 412 homes at about $1,200 each against 165 subscribers paying $70 a month, a payback near 3.6 years. The <strong>project charter</strong> formally authorizes the project and gives the project manager authority to apply resources. One or two pages: purpose and measurable objectives, high-level scope and exclusions, milestones, budget, key risks, assumptions and constraints, success criteria, the PM's name and authority, and the sponsor's signature. Work started without a signed charter is work nobody agreed to pay for.</p>
<h4>Stakeholders</h4>
<p>List everyone who affects or is affected by the job, then analyze them. The <strong>power-interest grid</strong> gives four strategies: high power and high interest, <em>manage closely</em> (the ISP sponsor); high power and low interest, <em>keep satisfied</em> (the city's right-of-way office, the pole owner); low power and high interest, <em>keep informed</em> (residents whose lawns you are boring); low on both, <em>monitor</em>. Record each one's current and desired engagement (unaware, resistant, neutral, supportive, leading) in the stakeholder register and plan the moves. An HOA that learns about the bore from a drill rig in the driveway goes from neutral to resistant in one morning.</p>
<h4>Requirements, scope and the WBS</h4>
<p>Requirements state what must be true at the end (every home within reach of a terminal, splice loss within the owner's spec, a portal that activates service in under ten minutes), each traceable to a stakeholder and to a test. The scope statement lists deliverables, acceptance criteria and exclusions; the exclusions prevent most arguments. Scope is then decomposed into a <strong>work breakdown structure</strong>: a deliverable-oriented hierarchy obeying the 100% rule (the children contain all of the parent's work and nothing else), broken down until each work package can be estimated, assigned and checked. A WBS dictionary describes every package.</p>
<pre>FTTH Oak Hollow                Provisioning portal
1 Project management           1 Project management
2 Design and permits           2 Requirements and design
  2.1 Walkout   2.2 Prints       2.1 Stories  2.2 API design
  2.3 Permits   2.4 Make-ready 3 Build
3 Underground                    3.1 Login  3.2 Activation
  3.1 Locates   3.2 Bore         3.3 UI
  3.3 Handholes 3.4 Cable      4 Test: unit, integration, UAT
4 Aerial  5 Splicing           5 Deploy and hand over
6 Test, as-builts  7 Restore</pre>
<h4>Estimating</h4>
<p>Analogous estimates borrow from a similar past job; parametric ones multiply a rate (24,000 ft of bore ÷ 1,200 ft a day from two rigs = 20 days); bottom-up ones sum the work packages. A <strong>three-point estimate</strong> takes optimistic, most likely and pessimistic values (O, M, P): the triangular mean is (O+M+P)/3 and the PERT mean (O+4M+P)/6, with σ = (P−O)/6. For the bore, O = 17, M = 20, P = 32 gives (17+80+32)/6 = 21.5 days with σ = 2.5. Along a path the means add and so do the variances, so a path's σ is the square root of the sum of the squared σs. Agile teams estimate in <strong>story points</strong>, relative size on a Fibonacci-like scale, converted to time only through measured velocity (Module 5). Then take the outside view. <strong>Reference-class forecasting</strong>, Kahneman and Tversky's correction for the planning fallacy and Flyvbjerg's tool in practice, checks your estimate against the actual outcomes of similar past jobs: if your last nine bores finished at a median of 1.12 and an 80th percentile of about 1.33 times their estimates, an 80%-confident commitment for the 20-day bore is 27 days.</p>
<h4>Scheduling and the critical path</h4>
<p>List the activities, link them (finish-to-start by default; start-to-start, finish-to-finish, leads and lags exist) and draw the network. The forward pass computes earliest start and finish (ES, EF); the backward pass computes latest start and finish (LS, LF); total float = LS − ES. The <strong>critical path</strong> is the longest path through the network, the zero-float chain: slip anything on it and the finish slips.</p>
<table><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Days</th><th>After</th><th>ES–EF</th><th>LS–LF</th><th>Float</th></tr>
<tr><td>A Permits, locates</td><td>10</td><td>—</td><td>0–10</td><td>0–10</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>B Bore, conduit</td><td>20</td><td>A</td><td>10–30</td><td>10–30</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>C Set handholes</td><td>6</td><td>A</td><td>10–16</td><td>24–30</td><td>14</td></tr>
<tr><td>D Place cable</td><td>8</td><td>B, C</td><td>30–38</td><td>30–38</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>E Aerial strand, lash</td><td>12</td><td>A</td><td>10–22</td><td>26–38</td><td>16</td></tr>
<tr><td>F Splice</td><td>10</td><td>D, E</td><td>38–48</td><td>38–48</td><td>0</td></tr>
<tr><td>G Test, as-builts</td><td>4</td><td>F</td><td>48–52</td><td>48–52</td><td>0</td></tr></table>
<p>The critical path A–B–D–F–G is 52 working days; handholes can slip 14 days and aerial 16 without moving the finish. To finish sooner, <em>crash</em> (add resources to critical work: a third rig cuts B to 14 days and the job to 46, at a price) or <em>fast-track</em> (overlap activities, such as placing cable behind the bore, at the risk of rework). Crashing B pays only until B reaches 6 days; below that A–C–D–F–G (38 days) becomes critical and extra rigs buy nothing. A <strong>Gantt chart</strong> draws the same schedule as bars on a calendar, milestones as diamonds, the critical path in red: the picture every customer understands.</p>
<h4>Budget and baselines</h4>
<p>Sum the work-package costs, add a <strong>contingency reserve</strong> for identified risks (sized in Module 4) and you have the cost baseline; the management reserve for unknown unknowns sits outside it and needs the sponsor. Oak Hollow: $430,000 of work plus $43,000 contingency is a $473,000 baseline; $21,400 of management reserve makes the $494,400 budget. The approved scope, schedule and cost baselines are what Module 3 measures against, and they change only through change control. Unapproved additions are <strong>scope creep</strong>.</p>
<h4>Exam mapping</h4>
<p>CAPM Domain 1 task 2 (planning), Domain 2 task 2 (the schedule) and Domain 4 tasks 2–3 (stakeholder communication, gathering requirements) are this module; PERT, float and critical-path questions are near-certain. Project+ places the charter, stakeholder register, WBS, schedule and budget in Domain 2.0 (life-cycle phases, 30%) and Domain 3.0 (tools and documentation, 19%).</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>From estimate to commitment on the Oak Hollow bore.</strong> Parametric: 24,000 ft ÷ (2 rigs × 600 ft a day) = 20 days. Three-point: the foremen give O = 17 (all dirt), M = 20, P = 32 (the limestone shelf on the east side), so PERT = 21.5 days, σ = 2.5, and roughly 84% confidence at mean + 1σ = 24 days. Outside view: the company's last nine bores finished at 0.95, 1.02, 1.05, 1.10, 1.12, 1.18, 1.30, 1.38 and 1.65 times estimate; the median is 1.12 and the 80th percentile (the 7.4th value in order) is about 1.33, so P80 = 20 × 1.33 ≈ 27 days. The inside view says 24, the outside view says 27, and the outside view has the better track record. The charter states the bore as "20 days planned, 27 days committed at 80% confidence", the three extra rig-days of risk go into the contingency reserve rather than into a padded estimate, and the critical path tells you that every one of those days lands on the finish date, while 14 days of handhole float let you move the handhole crew wherever the bore needs help.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Padding every estimate is the safe way to plan.\" Hidden padding gets spent (Parkinson's law) and destroys your reference class; estimate honestly, then hold risk in an explicit, visible contingency reserve.","\"The critical path is the most important or most difficult work.\" It is simply the longest chain of dependent activities, the zero-float path; a trivial task can be critical and a hard one can have weeks of float.","\"The WBS is a to-do list of activities.\" It is a deliverable-oriented decomposition obeying the 100% rule; activities and their sequence come afterwards, in the schedule."],
problems:[
{q:"Splicing is estimated at O = 4, M = 6, P = 14 days. Compute the PERT mean and σ. Then find σ for a path of three activities with σ = 1, 2 and 2 days.", a:"Mean = (4 + 24 + 14) ÷ 6 = 7.0 days; σ = (14 − 4) ÷ 6 ≈ 1.67 days. Path σ = √(1 + 4 + 4) = 3 days (variances add, not σs)."},
{q:"In the Oak Hollow network, make-ready delays push aerial (E) 10 days late. Does the finish move? What changed?", a:"No: E has 16 days of float, so the finish stays at day 52, but only 6 days of float remain on E; flag it, because the next slip of more than 6 days makes A–E–F–G critical."},
{q:"A third rig costs $33,600 and cuts B from 20 to 14 days. The plan is 9 days late against a contract with $4,000 a day in liquidated damages. Crash or not?", a:"Crashing saves 6 days, or $24,000 of damages, for $33,600, so not on damages alone; it pays only if earlier revenue or other penalties are worth more than $9,600. Look at fast-tracking cable placement behind the bore first."},
{q:"Place each on the power-interest grid with a strategy: the city right-of-way office, residents on the bore route, the ISP's marketing VP planning the launch, the pole owner, a utility whose duct you will not touch.", a:"ROW office and pole owner: high power, low interest, keep satisfied. Residents: low power, high interest, keep informed. Marketing VP: high power, high interest, manage closely. The untouched utility: low and low, monitor."},
{q:"A draft WBS reads: 2 Design, 3 Bore, 4 Weekly meetings, 5 Bore rework. Diagnose three problems.", a:"Meetings belong under project management, not as a deliverable; 'rework' is not a deliverable (it is a quality or risk outcome); and the 100% rule fails because splicing, testing, as-builts and restoration are missing."}
],
checks:[{q:"What does a project charter do?",a:"Formally authorizes the project and gives the PM authority to apply resources."},{q:"Formula for total float?",a:"LS − ES (equivalently LF − EF)."},{q:"PERT mean and standard deviation?",a:"(O + 4M + P) ÷ 6 and (P − O) ÷ 6."}],
explain:"Teach ADA the forward and backward pass on the Oak Hollow network without notes, then explain why 'B slips two days' and 'E slips two days' get different answers, and why reference-class forecasting beats asking the foreman to try harder."},


{id:"p45m3", title:"Executing and Controlling: Earned Value, Change, Quality, Contracts and Status", minutes:60,
outcomes:["Compute and interpret PV, EV, AC, CV, SV, CPI, SPI, three EACs, ETC, VAC and TCPI.","Turn daily as-builts and production reports into earned value and unit-price invoices.","Run a change through integrated change control, from field change to signed change order.","Distinguish quality assurance from quality control and reason with the cost of quality.","Choose contract types and produce a communications plan and a one-page status report."],
body:`<p>It is day 10 of a 20-day bore. Are you winning, and could you prove it in one line before the sponsor asks? Execution is where plans meet dirt; control is the discipline of knowing, in numbers, how far reality has drifted and what you are doing about it. You already produce the most important control document on the job every night: the as-built.</p>
<h4>Earned value management</h4>
<p><strong>Earned value</strong> management (EVM) compares three numbers at a status date. Planned value (PV) is the budgeted cost of the work scheduled by now; earned value (EV) is the budgeted cost of the work actually done; actual cost (AC) is what that work really cost. BAC is the budget at completion. The control account here is the Oak Hollow bore: BAC $240,000 for 24,000 ft at a budgeted $10 a foot over 20 days. By day 10 the plan called for 12,000 ft (PV $120,000), the as-builts show 10,200 ft (EV $102,000), and crews, rigs and material cost $112,000 (AC).</p>
<table><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Formula</th><th>Bore, day 10</th></tr>
<tr><td>Cost variance</td><td>CV = EV − AC</td><td>−$10,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Schedule variance</td><td>SV = EV − PV</td><td>−$18,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost performance index</td><td>CPI = EV ÷ AC</td><td>0.91: 91 cents of work per dollar</td></tr>
<tr><td>Schedule performance index</td><td>SPI = EV ÷ PV</td><td>0.85: 85% of planned pace</td></tr>
<tr><td>Estimate at completion</td><td>EAC = BAC ÷ CPI</td><td>≈ $263,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>Estimate to complete</td><td>ETC = EAC − AC</td><td>≈ $151,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>Variance at completion</td><td>VAC = BAC − EAC</td><td>≈ −$23,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>To-complete performance index</td><td>TCPI = (BAC − EV) ÷ (BAC − AC)</td><td>138,000 ÷ 128,000 = 1.08</td></tr></table>
<p>Read it aloud: behind schedule and over cost, and finishing on budget would require the rest of the bore to run 8% more efficiently than planned, which rarely happens without a change. Choose the EAC by cause. A one-time event already behind you (a day of rock): EAC = AC + (BAC − EV) = $250,000. Current efficiency continuing: BAC ÷ CPI ≈ $263,500. Cost and schedule pressure both persisting: AC + (BAC − EV) ÷ (CPI × SPI) ≈ $290,300. A rough duration forecast is 20 ÷ SPI ≈ 23.5 days. Late in a job SPI drifts back toward 1.0 because EV finally equals PV, which is why practitioners switch to earned schedule there.</p>
<h4>Daily production reporting: your as-built is the EV feed</h4>
<p>The Reel Installation As Builts sheet from Domain 24 is not paperwork; it is the measurement system. Footage installed comes from the sequential marks (slack plus running line), and under a unit-price contract those same quantities are the invoice. The daily production report adds what the as-built lacks: crew and hours, equipment, units completed (handholes set, splices, terminals), delays with causes (rock, a locate not ready, weather), safety items and near misses, photos and redlines. Production rate = footage ÷ crew-days, compared to plan every day, not at month end: a crew planned at 600 ft a day that posts 480 is at SPI 0.8 on that work today, and by lunch you know whether it was rock or a broken reamer. Rounded-up footage is not a shortcut; it corrupts EV, the invoice and the next estimate, and it is an ethics violation (Module 6).</p>
<h4>Change control</h4>
<p>Change is normal; uncontrolled change is how projects die. <strong>Change control</strong> runs every change through one path: request, impact analysis (scope, schedule, cost, quality, risk), a decision by the authority named in the plan (often a change control board), updates to baselines and plans, and communication. In the field the trigger is usually a field change: rock forces the bore across the street, the prints are redlined, and a change order is priced and signed, before the work whenever possible. Scope creep is unapproved scope; gold plating is the team adding extras nobody asked for. In agile work the backlog absorbs change by reordering, but in the predictive parts of a hybrid project the baseline still governs.</p>
<h4>Quality</h4>
<p>Quality is conformance to requirements and fitness for use; grade is a category (a 12-count drop and a 288-count feeder differ in grade, not quality). Quality assurance improves the process; quality control inspects the product: OTDR traces against the owner's splice-loss spec and power-meter readings against the loss budget (Domain 24), code review and tests against the Definition of Done (Domain 20). The cost of quality is prevention and appraisal (training, inspection) versus failure (rework, truck rolls, warranty). A bad splice costs minutes at the closure, hours at acceptance testing, and a truck roll plus an angry customer after turn-up. Pareto charts find the few causes behind most defects; fishbone diagrams organize the search for root causes.</p>
<h4>Procurement and contracts</h4>
<table><tr><th>Contract</th><th>Cost risk sits with</th><th>Use when</th></tr>
<tr><td>Firm fixed price</td><td>Seller</td><td>Scope fully defined</td></tr>
<tr><td>Unit price</td><td>Shared: rate fixed, quantity varies</td><td>Per foot bored, per splice, per handhole</td></tr>
<tr><td>Time and materials</td><td>Mostly buyer; cap with a not-to-exceed</td><td>Small or unclear scope, emergency restoration</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost plus fee</td><td>Buyer</td><td>High uncertainty, research-like work</td></tr></table>
<p>Most fiber work runs under a master service agreement with task orders and a <strong>unit-price contract</strong> rate card; software vendors sell fixed-price statements of work or T&amp;M hours. Read the clauses that bite: changed conditions (rock), payment terms and retainage, liquidated damages, insurance, and what counts as acceptance.</p>
<h4>Communications and status</h4>
<p>A communications plan says who gets what, when, how and from whom. Channels grow as n(n−1)/2, so 12 stakeholders means 66 possible channels, which is why you plan them. A status report fits on one page: overall red, amber or green against thresholds set in advance, the numbers (SPI, CPI, footage against plan), accomplishments, next period, top risks and issues from the risk register and issue log, and decisions needed by a date. Bad news goes early, with options.</p>
<h4>Exam mapping</h4>
<p>CAPM Domain 1 task 4 (executing planned strategies such as communications) and Domain 2 task 3 (documenting controls on predictive projects) live here, and EVM formulas are a CAPM staple. Project+ tests change control, communication plans, contract types, quality and status reporting across Domains 1.0 to 3.0.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Monday's status from Friday's as-builts.</strong> Last week's plan: 3,000 ft of bore (5 days × 600 ft) at $10 a foot, PV $30,000. The reconciled as-builts total 2,520 ft (first IN minus last OUT agrees with slack plus running line), so EV = $25,200; payroll, rig and material came to AC = $27,900. CPI = 25,200 ÷ 27,900 = 0.90; SPI = 25,200 ÷ 30,000 = 0.84: amber on both against thresholds of 0.95 and 0.85. The notes column explains it: rock from STA 14+00 to 17+50, 350 ft drilled at about a third of normal speed. Three actions follow. A change order under the contract's changed-conditions clause for the 350 ft of rock at the agreed rock adder of $18 a foot ($6,300, with production logs attached as evidence). A risk-register update raising the rock probability on the next two segments, which sit on the same shelf. And a status line the sponsor can act on: "Bore amber: SPI 0.84, CPI 0.90 after 350 ft of rock; change order CO-07 for $6,300 submitted; recovering two days by moving the handhole crew (14 days of float) to support the bore; decision needed by Wednesday on test-boring segment 4."</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"We've spent half the budget, so we're half done.\" Spending is AC; percent complete is EV ÷ BAC. Only earned value tells you how much work the money bought.","\"An SPI of 1.0 at the end proves we caught up.\" SPI returns to 1.0 at completion by construction; compare the actual finish with the baseline date or use earned schedule.","\"Fixed price is always safest for the buyer.\" Only with a well-defined scope; otherwise the seller prices the risk in and every unclear item becomes a change order."],
problems:[
{q:"BAC $80,000, PV $40,000, EV $36,000, AC $42,000. Compute CV, SV, CPI, SPI, EAC (BAC ÷ CPI) and TCPI, and say what they mean together.", a:"CV = −$6,000; SV = −$4,000; CPI ≈ 0.857; SPI = 0.90; EAC ≈ $93,333; TCPI = 44,000 ÷ 38,000 ≈ 1.16. Over cost and slightly behind; hitting budget would need 16% better efficiency, so re-forecast and report the EAC rather than promise the BAC."},
{q:"The team grows from 8 to 14 people. How many additional communication channels does that create?", a:"8 × 7 ÷ 2 = 28; 14 × 13 ÷ 2 = 91; 63 more channels, a reason to formalize the communications plan."},
{q:"Choose a contract type and justify it: (a) emergency restoration after a fiber cut with unknown damage; (b) a 9,000-ft aerial overlash on a known route; (c) a vendor building a provisioning API with unclear requirements.", a:"(a) Time and materials with a not-to-exceed cap. (b) Unit price or firm fixed price: scope and quantities are known. (c) T&M or sprint-based fixed price per increment; firm fixed price would force the vendor to price in huge risk."},
{q:"The as-built totals show 10,200 ft installed, but the daily reports add up to 11,050 ft. Diagnose it and say which number feeds EV and the invoice.", a:"A double count or transcription error in the daily reports. Reconcile with the sequential marks (first IN minus last OUT must equal slack plus running line, Domain 24); the reconciled as-built feeds EV and billing."},
{q:"The owner asks to add 18 homes on a cul-de-sac. Walk the change-control steps with a concrete impact.", a:"Log the request; analyze impact (+1,400 ft of bore at $10 = $14,000, about 1.2 bore days plus mobilization on the critical path, one more terminal, a permit amendment); decision by the CCB or sponsor; update scope, schedule and cost baselines; communicate to crews and stakeholders."}
],
checks:[{q:"CPI formula, and what does CPI below 1 mean?",a:"EV ÷ AC; the work is costing more than budgeted."},{q:"Which contract type puts most cost risk on the seller?",a:"Firm fixed price."},{q:"Communication channels formula?",a:"n(n − 1) ÷ 2."}],
explain:"Read ADA the bore's day-10 numbers and explain, in language a sponsor would accept, what each EVM index says, which EAC you would report and why, and how the nightly as-built feeds every number."},

{id:"p45m4", title:"Risk and Issues: Registers, EMV, Monte Carlo, Safety and Cyber", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Write cause–event–effect risk statements, keep a risk register and an issue log, and tell a risk from an issue.","Score risks qualitatively and compute EMV, decision trees and merge-bias probabilities; explain Monte Carlo schedule analysis.","Choose threat and opportunity responses and size contingency and management reserves.","Run a job hazard analysis with the hierarchy of controls and compute TRIR.","Connect project risk to cyber risk (ALE, threat models, authorized testing) from Domain 33."],
body:`<p>What could hurt this job, how likely is it, how much would it cost, and what are you doing about it today? Risk management is the part of project work closest to your security training: anticipate, quantify, prioritize, control and keep the evidence. The arithmetic is the same arithmetic Domain 33 used for security risk.</p>
<h4>Risks, issues and how to write them</h4>
<p>A risk is an uncertain event or condition that, if it occurs, affects an objective; it can be a threat or an opportunity. An issue is a risk that has already happened, or any current problem. Risks live in the <strong>risk register</strong> (ID, statement, probability, impact, score, owner, response, trigger, status); issues live in the <strong>issue log</strong> (description, owner, priority, date raised, due date, resolution). Write risks as cause, event, effect so someone can act on them: "Because the route crosses a limestone shelf, the bore may hit rock, cutting production 60% for 1,500 ft." Identify them by brainstorming with the crew (they know where the rock is), checklists built from past lessons learned, assumption analysis (every assumption in the charter is a risk), and interviews.</p>
<h4>Qualitative analysis</h4>
<p>Score probability and impact on 1–5 scales, multiply, and plot on a probability–impact matrix; the top right gets attention first. Define the scales in advance so "high" means the same to everyone: impact 5 might be more than $50,000, more than 10 days on the critical path, or any lost-time injury. Add urgency and an owner by name, not by department.</p>
<h4>Quantitative analysis</h4>
<p><strong>Expected monetary value</strong> (EMV) is probability × impact, summed over outcomes, with threats negative and opportunities positive.</p>
<table><tr><th>Risk</th><th>P</th><th>Impact</th><th>EMV</th></tr>
<tr><td>Rock on 1,500 ft of bore</td><td>0.30</td><td>−$36,000</td><td>−$10,800</td></tr>
<tr><td>Locates late, crew idle 2 days</td><td>0.50</td><td>−$8,000</td><td>−$4,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pole owner requires 3 replacements</td><td>0.25</td><td>−$22,500</td><td>−$5,625</td></tr>
<tr><td>Conduit price drop (opportunity)</td><td>0.20</td><td>+$5,000</td><td>+$1,000</td></tr></table>
<p>The threats sum to −$20,425, a defensible floor for the contingency reserve. Decision trees apply the same logic to choices: bore a 1,000-ft segment for $18,000 with a 30% chance of rock adding $12,000 (expected $21,600), or go aerial for $15,000 with a 40% chance of $15,000 in pole replacements (expected $21,000). Nearly tied, so schedule decides: make-ready can take months. Schedules carry their own risk. Where paths merge, on-time probabilities multiply: two parallel paths each 70% likely to be on time give 0.7 × 0.7 = 49% at the merge. Monte Carlo simulation handles all of it: give each activity a three-point distribution (Module 2), sample every activity thousands of times, compute the finish each time, and read the percentiles. Run 10,000 trials on the Oak Hollow network with right-skewed ranges and the 52-day plan built from most-likely durations comes out only about 6% likely; P50 is about 58 days and P80 about 62. Commit to percentiles, not to single numbers.</p>
<h4>Responses and reserves</h4>
<p>For threats: <em>avoid</em> (change the plan: route around the shelf), <em>transfer</em> (insurance, a rock clause, a specialist subcontract), <em>mitigate</em> (test-bore the shelf, carry rock tooling), <em>accept</em> (actively with contingency, or passively), or <em>escalate</em> beyond the project's authority. For opportunities the mirror set: exploiting, sharing, enhancing, accepting or escalating. Every response has an owner, a trigger and a residual risk, and some create secondary risks. The <strong>contingency reserve</strong> covers identified risks inside the cost baseline and is drawn down as they occur; the management reserve covers unknown unknowns outside the baseline and needs the sponsor. Oak Hollow's $43,000 of contingency covers $20,425 of quantified threat plus the unscored tail, and you can defend it line by line.</p>
<h4>Issues and lessons learned</h4>
<p>When a risk fires it moves to the issue log with an owner and a due date, and the daily meeting works the oldest open items first. Capture <strong>lessons learned</strong> throughout the job, not only at closeout: what happened, why, and what to do differently, written so the next estimator can use it. They feed the next job's risk checklists and its reference class (Module 2). A lessons-learned file nobody reads is a diary.</p>
<h4>Field safety is risk management</h4>
<p>A <strong>job hazard analysis</strong> (OSHA publication 3071) breaks a job into steps, lists each step's hazards and assigns controls from the hierarchy of controls: elimination, substitution, engineering controls, administrative controls, then PPE. Review it at the morning tailgate for that day's work. Fiber hazards: struck utilities (an 811 ticket, the legal wait, and hand-digging or vacuum excavation inside the tolerance zone around marks); excavations (5 ft or deeper generally needs a protective system under 29 CFR 1926 Subpart P); traffic (a work zone set up to the MUTCD); aerial work near power (fall protection, minimum approach distances); heat; and the fiber itself: never look into a fiber or connector, because invisible infrared laser light can burn the retina, and treat glass shards as sharps (black mat, sharps container, safety glasses, no food). Track leading indicators (JHAs done, near misses reported) as well as lagging ones: TRIR = recordable cases × 200,000 ÷ hours worked, so 3 recordables in 150,000 hours is 4.0.</p>
<h4>Cyber risk on technology projects</h4>
<p>Software and network projects carry security risks that belong in the same register: a vulnerability in a vendor component, a portal storing customer data, a contractor with remote access. Domain 33's ALE = SLE × ARO is EMV on a yearly clock, and its treatments are the responses above. Put security requirements and a threat model into scope during planning, not after launch. A penetration test is itself a project: a charter, a scope, written permission from the system owner, and rules of engagement that act as its scope statement.</p>
<h4>Exam mapping</h4>
<p>CAPM Domain 1 tasks 4 and 5 (executing risk strategies; problem-solving tools) cover EMV, matrices, responses and the risk-versus-issue distinction. Project+ Domain 1.0 tests risk strategies and the register; Domain 2.0 places risk work in the life cycle.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Pricing risk into the Oak Hollow plan.</strong> The register holds 14 risks; the four quantified above sum to −$20,425 EMV, so the $43,000 contingency (10% of work) is not padding but about 2.1 times the quantified threat, covering the unscored long tail. A spreadsheet Monte Carlo (one row per trial, each activity drawn from its triangular O–M–P range, finish = the longest path) run 10,000 times gives P50 ≈ 58 and P80 ≈ 62 working days against the 52-day most-likely plan, so the charter's customer milestone is set at day 62 and the crew plan at day 52; the gap is schedule contingency, owned by you and visible to the sponsor. The JHA for "excavate bore pit at STA 12+50" lists three steps and their controls: verify the 811 ticket and visible marks, then vacuum-excavate inside the tolerance zone (administrative, engineering); keep the pit under 5 ft or install a trench box (engineering); and set the MUTCD lane-closure layout before the truck stops, with hard hats, vests and glasses (administrative, PPE). Cost of those controls: about $1,900 a day. Cost of one struck gas line: incalculable.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"The risk register is a kickoff form.\" It is a living control: reviewed weekly, owners act on triggers, and scores change as you learn.","\"Contingency is padding.\" Contingency is sized from identified risks (EMV), visible and drawn down when they occur; padding is hidden in estimates and gets spent anyway.","\"Safety is the safety manager's job.\" The JHA is planning, the person running the job owns the hazards in the plan, and anyone on the crew can stop work."],
problems:[
{q:"Net EMV: 40% chance of a $25,000 delay penalty, 10% chance of $80,000 rework, and a 30% chance of a $10,000 early-completion bonus.", a:"−10,000 − 8,000 + 3,000 = −$15,000."},
{q:"Option A: fixed quote $50,000. Option B: T&M estimated at $40,000 with a 30% chance of $25,000 more. Which has the lower expected cost, and what else decides?", a:"B: 40,000 + 0.3 × 25,000 = $47,500 versus $50,000. But B's worst case is $65,000; the buyer's risk appetite and cash position decide whether $2,500 of expected saving is worth that spread."},
{q:"Three parallel paths, each 80% likely on time, merge into splicing. What is the probability that splicing can start on time?", a:"0.8³ = 0.512, about 51% (merge bias, assuming independence)."},
{q:"A crew of 40 works 50 hours a week for 50 weeks and records 2 recordable injuries. TRIR?", a:"Hours = 40 × 50 × 50 = 100,000; TRIR = 2 × 200,000 ÷ 100,000 = 4.0."},
{q:"Name the response type: (a) buy rock tooling and test-bore the shelf; (b) subcontract the highway crossing at a fixed price to a specialist; (c) reroute the segment aerial on existing poles; (d) note the risk and hold contingency.", a:"(a) Mitigate. (b) Transfer. (c) Avoid. (d) Accept, actively."}
],
checks:[{q:"Risk versus issue?",a:"A risk is uncertain and future; an issue is happening now."},{q:"EMV formula?",a:"Probability × impact, summed over outcomes."},{q:"Hierarchy of controls, most to least effective?",a:"Elimination, substitution, engineering, administrative, PPE."}],
explain:"Let ADA play a sponsor who says 'contingency is padding, cut it'. Defend the reserve with the EMV table and the Monte Carlo P80, then explain to ADA why the job hazard analysis belongs in the same conversation."},


{id:"p45m5", title:"Agile and Hybrid Delivery: Scrum, Kanban, Flow, Scaling and When Agile Is Wrong", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Apply the 2020 Scrum Guide precisely: accountabilities, events and timeboxes, artifacts and commitments.","Use Kanban flow metrics and Little's law to set WIP limits and predict cycle time.","Forecast with velocity ranges and percent plan complete, and explain why velocity must never become a target.","Judge SAFe, LeSS and Nexus honestly and design a hybrid plan joining construction to software sprints.","Run retrospectives that produce owned, measured changes, and recognize when agile is the wrong choice."],
body:`<p>When do you plan the whole job up front, when do you plan two weeks at a time, and how do you run both on one project without the halves fighting? Domain 20 taught Scrum and Kanban from inside a software team. This module takes the PM's seat: forecasting with them, scaling them, bolting them to a construction schedule, and knowing when they are the wrong tool.</p>
<h4>Scrum in depth</h4>
<p>The Scrum Guide (Schwaber and Sutherland, November 2020) is still the official text; Jeff Sutherland's 2025 Expansion Pack is a supplement, not a replacement. Scrum rests on empiricism (transparency, inspection, adaptation) and lean thinking. Three accountabilities: the Product Owner maximizes value and orders the Product Backlog; the Scrum Master is accountable for the team's effectiveness; the Developers build a usable Increment every <strong>Sprint</strong>. Five events, all timeboxed: the Sprint itself (one month or less), Sprint Planning (at most eight hours for a one-month Sprint), the Daily Scrum (15 minutes, for the Developers), the Sprint Review (at most four hours) and the Sprint Retrospective (at most three hours), each usually shorter for shorter Sprints. Three artifacts, each with a commitment: Product Backlog and the Product Goal, Sprint Backlog and the Sprint Goal, Increment and the <strong>Definition of Done</strong>. Refinement is ongoing work, not an event. The anti-patterns you will meet: a Daily Scrum run as a status report to a manager, a Product Owner without authority, Sprints that are mini-waterfalls ending in "testing next Sprint," and "Done" that means "coded."</p>
<h4>Kanban and flow</h4>
<p>Kanban visualizes work on a board, limits work in progress per column, and manages flow. Its four measures are WIP, <strong>throughput</strong> (items finished per unit of time), work item age and cycle time (start to finish). <strong>Little's law</strong> ties them together for a stable system: average cycle time = average WIP ÷ average throughput. A splicing crew with 12 closures in progress finishing 4 a day has a 3-day cycle time; set a <strong>WIP limit</strong> of 8 and, at the same throughput, cycle time falls to 2 days with nobody working harder. A software team with 15 tickets open finishing 5 a week makes each ticket wait 3 weeks. The law holds for long-run averages, consistent units, and arrivals roughly equal to departures; it is a diagnostic, not a promise. A cumulative flow diagram shows WIP as band thickness, and a cycle-time scatterplot yields a service-level expectation such as "85% of tickets finish within 6 days."</p>
<h4>Velocity and forecasting</h4>
<p><strong>Velocity</strong> is the story points a team finishes per Sprint. Forecast with a range, never a single number: the last six Sprints were 21, 18, 25, 20, 23 and 19 (mean 21, range 18–25), so a remaining backlog of 160 points needs 160 ÷ 25 to 160 ÷ 18, or 6.4 to 8.9 Sprints: "seven to nine." Velocity belongs to one team. It is meaningless across teams and destroyed the moment it becomes a target, because points inflate as soon as they are rewarded (Goodhart's law).</p>
<h4>Scaling, honestly</h4>
<p>SAFe (Scaled Agile, Inc.) groups 50–125 people into Agile Release Trains that plan together every 8–12 weeks in PI Planning, adding roles and a portfolio layer; it is common in large enterprises and government, and criticized as heavy and agile in name only. LeSS (Larman and Vodde) keeps one Product Owner and one Product Backlog for up to about eight teams and adds as little as possible. Nexus (Scrum.org) and Scrum@Scale are lighter options. No scaling model fixes tangled architecture or cross-team dependencies, and the evidence for each is mostly case studies and vendor surveys. Remove dependencies first; scale the process last.</p>
<h4>Hybrid: construction plus software</h4>
<p>An ISP opening a market runs two kinds of work at once. The plant (permits, bores, splices) is predictive: a CPM schedule, unit-price contracts, earned value. The provisioning portal and its billing and inventory integrations are adaptive: two-week Sprints, a Product Owner from the ISP, a backlog. Synchronize at milestones that matter to both, such as "first serving area lit," the date the portal must activate a real customer. Construction has its own pull-planning method, the Last Planner System (Lean Construction Institute): foremen commit to a weekly work plan drawn from a three-to-six-week look-ahead, and percent plan complete (PPC) = assignments completed ÷ assignments planned, so 18 of 24 is 75%. It is a Sprint commitment in a hard hat.</p>
<h4>Retrospectives that change behavior</h4>
<p>A retrospective earns its hour only if behavior changes. Use Derby and Larsen's five steps (set the stage, gather data, generate insights, decide what to do, close), open by checking the last action, and leave with one or two specific actions, each with an owner, a date and a measure. The 2020 Guide lets the most impactful improvement go straight into the next Sprint Backlog, where it competes for capacity like any other work. Psychological safety (Module 6) decides whether the real problem is ever said aloud; capture what you learn as lessons learned the next project can use.</p>
<h4>When agile is wrong</h4>
<p>Agile wins when requirements are uncertain and feedback is cheap. It loses when scope is fixed by physics, permits or contract and change is expensive (the bore under the highway), when no customer will look at an increment, when the work is repetitive operations better served by Kanban and service levels (Domain 26), and when "agile" is an excuse not to plan. The Stacey matrix and Cynefin say the same: clear and complicated work wants plans and experts; complex work wants experiments.</p>
<h4>Exam mapping</h4>
<p>This is CAPM Domain 3 (20%): when to use an adaptive approach, planning iterations, adaptive controls, the components of an adaptive plan, and task management on boards. Scrum.org's PSM I tests the Scrum Guide itself, so learn the timeboxes and commitments word for word. Project+ Domain 1.0 tests agile concepts and when to choose them.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Will the portal be ready when the first area lights?</strong> The plant's critical path puts serving area 1 spliced and tested on working day 40. The portal's minimum viable product is 96 points of backlog; Sprints are 10 working days; the last four velocities were 22, 18, 26 and 20. Forecast: 96 ÷ 26 = 3.7 to 96 ÷ 18 = 5.3, so 4 to 6 Sprints, or day 40 to day 60. Only the optimistic end makes the milestone, so the PM and the Product Owner cut scope rather than hope: activation, payment and install scheduling stay; the referral program and the speed-upgrade flow move to release 2. The MVP drops to 72 points, and 72 ÷ 18 = 4 Sprints even at the worst recent velocity: day 40. Meanwhile the splicing crew's Kanban board shows 12 closures in progress at 4 a day, a 3-day cycle time; a WIP limit of 8 brings it to 2 days, so the last closures in area 1 finish a day earlier, which becomes the integration test window for the portal. Two methods, one milestone, both forecasts written as ranges.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Velocity measures productivity, so higher is better.\" It is one team's planning number; compare it across teams or reward it and story points inflate.","\"The Daily Scrum is the team's status report to the Scrum Master.\" It is the Developers' 15-minute event to inspect progress toward the Sprint Goal and adapt their plan.","\"Hybrid just means doing Scrum badly.\" Hybrid means choosing the right approach for each workstream and synchronizing at milestones; a predictive plant schedule beside a sprinting software team is hybrid done right."],
problems:[
{q:"A ticket queue has a throughput of 6 a day and an average cycle time of 4 days. What is the average WIP? If you impose a WIP limit of 12, what cycle time should you expect?", a:"WIP = 6 × 4 = 24. With WIP 12 at the same throughput, cycle time = 12 ÷ 6 = 2 days."},
{q:"Velocities: 30, 24, 27, 33, 26. Remaining backlog: 210 points. Give the forecast range in Sprints.", a:"210 ÷ 33 ≈ 6.4 and 210 ÷ 24 ≈ 8.75, so 7 to 9 Sprints."},
{q:"Foremen committed to 30 assignments last week and completed 21. Compute PPC and name two causes to investigate.", a:"PPC = 21 ÷ 30 = 70%. Investigate constraints not cleared before commitment (materials, locates, permits, equipment) and over-commitment from an unrealistic look-ahead."},
{q:"Design the delivery approach for a data-center fiber retrofit that also needs a monitoring dashboard and a stream of moves, adds and changes.", a:"Predictive for the cabling (fixed racks and pathways, change windows, CPM), Kanban with WIP limits for the moves/adds/changes queue, Scrum for the dashboard; synchronize at cutover."},
{q:"A team's retrospective action reads 'communicate better.' Rewrite it so it can change behavior.", a:"Example: 'Starting Monday, the Developers post blockers in the team channel within one hour of hitting them; owner Ana; measure: median blocker age at the next retro, target under four hours.' Specific, owned, dated, measured, and placed in the next Sprint Backlog."}
],
checks:[{q:"The three Scrum artifacts and their commitments?",a:"Product Backlog with the Product Goal, Sprint Backlog with the Sprint Goal, Increment with the Definition of Done."},{q:"Little's law?",a:"Average cycle time = average WIP ÷ average throughput."},{q:"Daily Scrum timebox?",a:"15 minutes."}],
explain:"Explain to ADA how you would run the Oak Hollow plant and its provisioning portal as one hybrid project (what is predictive, what is agile, where they sync), then let ADA argue for 'just do it all in Scrum' and answer it."},

{id:"p45m6", title:"Leading People: Influence, Feedback, Conflict, Negotiation and Ethics", minutes:55,
outcomes:["Distinguish leadership from management and lead without formal authority.","Match leadership style to development level and team stage.","Apply motivation research (self-determination theory, Herzberg) to crews and software teams.","Run meetings, one-on-ones and SBI feedback; choose a conflict mode; negotiate with a BATNA and a ZOPA.","Build psychological safety, manage up, and apply PMI's Code of Ethics to field dilemmas."],
body:`<p>Why will a crew work hard for one foreman and drag for another with the same pay, the same trucks and the same dirt? Because projects are delivered by people, and the plans in Modules 2–4 are only as good as the willingness of the people carrying them out. Leadership is learnable, and most of it is a handful of habits practiced consistently.</p>
<h4>Leadership, management and power</h4>
<p>John Kotter's distinction (<em>Harvard Business Review</em>, 1990) still works: management copes with complexity (planning, budgeting, organizing, controlling) and leadership copes with change (setting direction, aligning people, motivating them). A PM needs both, usually without much formal authority: the bore crew belongs to a subcontractor and the developers report to an engineering manager. French and Raven's five bases of power show what you have instead: legitimate (the title), reward, coercive, expert (you know the work) and referent (people trust you). Expert and referent power are the ones a former technician brings, and the ones that last.</p>
<h4>Situational leadership and team stages</h4>
<p><strong>Situational leadership</strong> (Hersey and Blanchard) matches your style to a person's development level on a specific task. A new hire on a fusion splicer gets <em>directing</em>: show, then watch. Someone learning but frustrated gets <em>coaching</em>: direction plus the reasons. A capable tech shaken by two failed OTDR acceptances gets <em>supporting</em>: listen, ask, encourage. A veteran gets <em>delegating</em>: the outcome, the deadline and your trust. The same person can need delegating on bores and directing on splicing. Teams move through Tuckman's stages (forming, storming, norming, performing, and adjourning at the end): clarify roles while forming, surface conflict while storming, then get out of the way.</p>
<h4>Motivation</h4>
<p>Domain 46's self-determination theory applies directly: people are driven by autonomy, competence and relatedness. Herzberg's two-factor theory adds a field truth: hygiene factors (pay on time, working tools, water in the truck, a sane schedule) do not motivate, but their absence demotivates fast; the motivators are achievement, recognition and responsibility. Fix hygiene first, then make progress visible (a footage board at the yard), let the crew decide how to hit the target, and recognize specific contributions in public.</p>
<h4>Meetings, one-on-ones and feedback</h4>
<p>Every meeting needs a purpose, an agenda, a decision owner and a timebox, and ends with actions written as who, what, when. Hold a 10–15 minute tailgate in the field that walks the day's job hazard analysis, and a one-on-one with each direct report every week or two, about 30 minutes, their agenda first; Andy Grove's <em>High Output Management</em> (1983) ranks it among a manager's highest-leverage hours. Give feedback with SBI, the Center for Creative Leadership's situation–behavior–impact model: "At the Elm Street pit this morning, you started digging before the locate marks were checked. If that had been gas, people would have been hurt, and the ticket would have been void." Then ask, listen and agree the next step. Praise in public, correct in private, both within a day.</p>
<h4>Conflict and negotiation</h4>
<p>Projects generate conflict over schedules, priorities, resources, technical choices and personalities, and schedules top most surveys of project conflict. The five modes (Thomas–Kilmann; the PMBOK names are withdraw/avoid, smooth/accommodate, compromise/reconcile, force/direct, collaborate/problem-solve) each have a place: collaborate when the issue and the relationship both matter; compromise under time pressure; accommodate when the other side cares more; avoid when emotions need a day; force when safety is at stake, which is what stop-work authority is. Negotiate on principle (Fisher and Ury, <em>Getting to Yes</em>): separate the people from the problem, focus on interests rather than positions, invent options for mutual gain, insist on objective criteria, and know your <strong>BATNA</strong>, your best alternative to a negotiated agreement. The zone of possible agreement (ZOPA) lies between the two sides' walk-away points.</p>
<h4>Managing up and teams you cannot see</h4>
<p>Your sponsor is your most important stakeholder: learn their goals and preferred channel, deliver no surprises, bring each problem with options and a recommendation, and confirm decisions in writing. Place your own boss on the power-interest grid; the answer is always manage closely. Field and remote teams need the same discipline written down: a morning tailgate or async stand-up, a shared folder of current prints and redlines, photos as evidence, decisions recorded where everyone can find them, and enough overlapping hours for a distributed software team to unblock each other.</p>
<h4>Psychological safety</h4>
<p><strong>Psychological safety</strong> is a shared belief that the team is safe for interpersonal risk-taking: admitting a mistake, reporting a near miss, saying "I don't know" (Amy Edmondson, 1999). Google's Project Aristotle found it the most important of five dynamics in its effective teams. It is not niceness: high standards with high safety produce learning, and high standards without it produce hidden problems. Leaders build it by framing work as a learning problem, admitting their own errors and thanking the person who brings bad news. On a crew it is the difference between a near-miss report and an injury.</p>
<h4>Ethics</h4>
<p>PMI's Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct rests on four values (responsibility, respect, fairness, honesty), each with aspirational and mandatory standards, and violations can cost a credential. In practice: never round up footage, never report green when it is amber, disclose a conflict of interest (the vendor offering a weekend trip), report unsafe work, and treat every person on the job with respect.</p>
<h4>Exam mapping</h4>
<p>CAPM Domain 1 task 3 (roles and responsibilities) and Domain 4 task 2 (stakeholder communication) draw on this module, and the PMP's People domain (33% of the exam since July 2026) is built on it. Project+ Domain 1.0 tests team dynamics, conflict, communication and the role's soft skills.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Negotiating the rock change order.</strong> Your unit rate is $10 a foot, but 1,500 ft of segment 4 is limestone, and your production logs show 180 ft a day instead of 600, which puts your true cost at $28 a foot: $42,000, your walk-away floor. The owner's BATNA is a rock specialist at $32 a foot plus $6,000 to mobilize and a two-week delay; at 165 subscribers × $70 ÷ 30 ≈ $385 a day of deferred revenue, 14 days costs $5,390. Their walk-away is therefore 48,000 + 6,000 + 5,390 = $59,390, about $39.60 a foot. The ZOPA runs from $28 to $39.60. You open with interests, not positions: they want the date and a defensible price; you want to cover cost and keep the customer. Objective criteria go on the table (the logs, the contract's changed-conditions clause, two published rock-boring rates). You settle at $31 a foot, $46,500, with the owner adding a second locator to that segment so the rig never waits. You clear $4,500 over cost; they save $12,890 against their BATNA and keep the date. Both sides can explain the number to their bosses, which is the test of a principled deal.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Leaders are born, not made.\" Leadership is a set of learnable behaviors (clear direction, fair feedback, consistent follow-through) practiced deliberately like any skill.","\"Psychological safety means lowering standards.\" It means people can raise problems without punishment; it works best paired with high standards.","\"Avoiding conflict keeps the team together.\" Unresolved conflict goes underground; collaborate on issues that matter, and force only for safety and ethics."],
problems:[
{q:"Choose a situational-leadership style for each: (a) an apprentice's first day pulling cable; (b) an experienced splicer, confident, using a new closure type; (c) a 15-year foreman running a standard bore; (d) a tech discouraged after two failed OTDR acceptances.", a:"(a) Directing. (b) Coaching: show the new steps and explain why, briefly. (c) Delegating. (d) Supporting: listen, rebuild confidence, little extra instruction needed."},
{q:"A seller's floor is $42,000 and the buyer's BATNA costs $59,390. Give the ZOPA and the midpoint, and say why the midpoint is not automatically fair.", a:"ZOPA $42,000 to $59,390; midpoint about $50,700. Fairness comes from objective criteria (cost records, market rates), not from splitting the difference."},
{q:"Pick a conflict mode: (a) two developers disagree on an API design that will last years; (b) a crew member removes a guard from a saw; (c) two foremen want the same truck on Friday; (d) a stakeholder is shouting in a public meeting.", a:"(a) Collaborate. (b) Force or direct, immediately: safety. (c) Compromise or accommodate. (d) Withdraw for now, then collaborate in private."},
{q:"Rewrite as SBI feedback: 'You're always late with the as-builts.'", a:"'This week's Tuesday and Thursday as-builts reached me Friday afternoon (situation, behavior). Billing missed its cutoff, so the invoice slips two weeks (impact). What got in the way?' Specific, factual, then a question."},
{q:"The owner's inspector tells you: 'Just put 1,200 ft on today's as-built; you'll make it up tomorrow.' Which Code of Ethics values are at stake, and what do you do?", a:"Honesty and responsibility. Record the actual footage, offer an accurate forecast instead, document the request, and escalate if the pressure continues."}
],
checks:[{q:"The four values of PMI's Code of Ethics?",a:"Responsibility, respect, fairness, honesty."},{q:"What does BATNA stand for?",a:"Best alternative to a negotiated agreement."},{q:"Tuckman's stages?",a:"Forming, storming, norming, performing, adjourning."}],
explain:"Role-play with ADA: ADA is a foreman who skipped this morning's job hazard analysis. Give SBI feedback, handle the pushback, then explain to ADA which leadership style and which conflict mode you used, and why."},


{id:"p45m7", title:"Tools, the Exams and the Career: From Technician to Project Manager", minutes:50,
outcomes:["Pick the right tool (Jira, Asana, Microsoft Project, Smartsheet, Primavera P6, ProjectLibre) for the job and write a useful Jira query.","Design a one-screen project dashboard with thresholds set in advance.","Define programs, portfolios and the three PMO types.","Map the CAPM content outline and the Project+ domains to this domain and follow a 10-week study plan.","Describe the path from technician to project manager with verified 2025 pay data and the PMP and PSM I as next steps."],
body:`<p>Which tools will you be handed, how do you pass the exams efficiently, and what is the path from the rig to the PM's desk, with real pay numbers? This module bridges knowing the material and being hired for it.</p>
<h4>The tools</h4>
<table><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Strength</th><th>Where you meet it</th></tr>
<tr><td>Jira (Atlassian)</td><td>Issues, Scrum and Kanban boards, Sprints, JQL queries, dashboards</td><td>Software and IT teams</td></tr>
<tr><td>Asana</td><td>Tasks, timelines, portfolios, light dependencies</td><td>Business and operations teams</td></tr>
<tr><td>Microsoft Project</td><td>A true scheduling engine: dependencies, resources, baselines, critical path</td><td>Construction, engineering, government</td></tr>
<tr><td>Smartsheet</td><td>Spreadsheet grid with Gantt view, forms and dashboards shared across companies</td><td>Telecom trackers: permits, production, subcontractors</td></tr>
<tr><td>Primavera P6 (Oracle)</td><td>Very large CPM schedules</td><td>Utilities, heavy construction</td></tr>
<tr><td>ProjectLibre</td><td>Free, open source desktop scheduler that opens Microsoft Project files</td><td>Your labs</td></tr></table>
<p>Choose by need. Real dependency logic and float require a scheduling engine (Project, P6, ProjectLibre); team workflow wants Jira; cross-company visibility favors a shared grid. Learn one query language well: in Jira, <code>project = FTTH AND statusCategory != Done AND duedate &lt; now() ORDER BY priority DESC</code> lists the overdue work for the morning meeting. A clean spreadsheet still beats a cluttered tool. AI assistants now draft status reports inside most of these tools; treat the output as a junior's first draft, because a confident hallucination in a status report is still a false report with your name on it.</p>
<h4>Dashboards</h4>
<p>A dashboard answers two questions at a glance: are we on track, and what needs a decision? For a fiber build: CPI and SPI against thresholds set before the job started (green at 0.95 or better, amber 0.85 to 0.95, red below), footage installed against the planned S-curve from earned value, homes passed and lit, the top five items from the risk register, open issues by age, pending change orders, and safety (near misses reported, days since a recordable). For the software stream: velocity range, burn-up against the release scope, and escaped defects. Prefer leading indicators, show trends rather than snapshots, and fit it on one screen.</p>
<h4>Programs, portfolios and the PMO</h4>
<p>A program groups related projects managed together for benefits none delivers alone: twelve serving areas, the portal and the marketing launch of a new city. A portfolio is everything an organization funds, projects, programs and operations, selected and balanced against strategy and capacity: which cities the ISP builds this year. PMI certifies those levels separately (PgMP, PfMP). A <strong>PMO</strong> standardizes how projects run, and PMI describes three types: supportive (templates, training, lessons learned), controlling (requires compliance with its methods) and directive (manages the projects itself); the PMBOK Eighth Edition expanded its PMO guidance. A good PMO makes the next project cheaper; a bad one is a reporting tax.</p>
<h4>The exams</h4>
<table><tr><th></th><th>PMI CAPM</th><th>CompTIA Project+ PK0-005</th></tr>
<tr><td>Format</td><td>150 questions (135 scored), 180 minutes</td><td>Up to 90 questions, 90 minutes, pass at 710 of 900</td></tr>
<tr><td>Entry</td><td>Secondary diploma and 23 hours of PM education</td><td>None; 6–12 months of experience recommended</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost, 2026</td><td>$225 PMI members, $300 others</td><td>About $399 list</td></tr>
<tr><td>Renewal</td><td>15 PDUs every 3 years</td><td>CompTIA CE every 3 years, since October 2025</td></tr></table>
<p>The CAPM content outline (2023 update) weights Fundamentals and Core Concepts at 36% (Modules 1–4 and 6), Predictive, Plan-Based Methodologies at 17% (Modules 2–3), the agile domain at 20% (Module 5) and Business Analysis at 27% (requirements, stakeholders, product roadmaps: Modules 2, 5 and 6). Candidates under-study business analysis; it is more than a quarter of the score. Project+ weights project management concepts 33%, life-cycle phases 30%, tools and documentation 19%, and IT and governance basics 18%, the last drawn from Domains 26 and 33. Your order: CAPM first, because it also satisfies the PMP's 35-hour education requirement; Project+ if an IT employer asks for CompTIA; Scrum.org's PSM I ($200, 80 questions in 60 minutes, 85% to pass, never expires) when you join a software team; then the PMP (180 questions in 240 minutes; People 33%, Process 41%, Business Environment 26% since 9 July 2026) once you have three to five years of leading projects, depending on your degree.</p>
<h4>A 10-week CAPM plan</h4>
<table><tr><th>Weeks</th><th>Study</th><th>Do</th></tr>
<tr><td>1–2</td><td>Modules 1–2 and the content outline</td><td>Lab 1</td></tr>
<tr><td>3–4</td><td>Modules 2–3: schedule, critical path, earned value</td><td>Labs 2–3</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Module 4</td><td>Lab 4</td></tr>
<tr><td>6–7</td><td>Module 5, plus requirements and roadmaps</td><td>Lab 5 (two Sprints)</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>Module 6</td><td>Lab 6</td></tr>
<tr><td>9–10</td><td>Timed mock exams, weakest domain first</td><td>Lab 7, then book the exam</td></tr></table>
<p>Seven hours a week is enough: two focus blocks on weeknights and one longer weekend session, the Recall deck daily, and every miss logged in the Mistake journal.</p>
<h4>From technician to PM, and the pay</h4>
<p>The field path runs technician, crew lead or foreman, field supervisor or superintendent, project coordinator or assistant PM, project manager, then construction or program manager. The IT path runs technician, team lead, project coordinator, then IT project manager, Scrum Master or delivery manager. US pay, BLS May 2025 medians: telecommunications line installers about $74,300; first-line construction supervisors $79,920; project management specialists $102,320 (bottom tenth under $61,580, top tenth over $167,970; $98,730 in construction); construction managers $114,990 (top tenth over $189,440). PMI's <em>Earning Power</em> survey (14th edition, November 2025, 14,628 respondents in 21 countries) put the US median for PMP holders at $135,000 against $109,157 without, about 24% more. Medians are not offers; region, employer and the size of the jobs you can prove decide your number. Start building proof now: own your crew's production report, keep a three-week look-ahead, write the JHA, track footage against the critical path, and log lessons learned. Those are the projects you will describe on a PMP application.</p>
<h4>Exam mapping</h4>
<p>Project+ Domain 3.0 (tools and documentation, 19%) and Domain 4.0 (IT and governance basics, 18%) are covered here together with Domains 26 and 33; the CAPM tests program, portfolio and PMO definitions within Domain 1.</p>`,
example:`<p><strong>Your CAPM budget and exam-day math.</strong> PMI membership is $139 a year plus a one-time $10 application fee, $149 the first year, and it drops the CAPM fee from $300 to $225 and a retake from $200 to $150: $374 all-in versus $300 without, so membership pays only if you value the member download of the PMBOK Guide, a possible retake discount, or plan the PMP later. The 23 education hours can come from PMI's on-demand CAPM Exam Prep course or from the Google Project Management Certificate on Coursera (six courses, $49 a month in the US, which Google says qualifies for 100-plus hours of PMI-recognized education); at three months that is $147. Exam day: 150 questions in 180 minutes is 1.2 minutes each, so checkpoints at question 50 by minute 60 and question 100 by minute 120. Flag anything that takes over two minutes and come back. Score your mocks by domain, not just in total: a 70% overall built on 55% in Business Analysis is a failing plan, because that domain is 27% of the real exam.</p>`,
misconceptions:["\"Learning Jira or MS Project makes you a project manager.\" Tools encode the method; without a WBS, dependencies and baselines, a tool just draws a prettier wrong plan.","\"CAPM is useless because it is entry-level.\" It proves the vocabulary, satisfies the PMP's 35-hour education requirement, and is the cheapest signal that you can move from field work to coordination.","\"The PMP needs a PM job title.\" PMI asks for experience leading projects, described by what you did, not by your title; crew leads who run look-aheads, production tracking and closeout are building it."],
problems:[
{q:"Write a Jira JQL query that lists every unresolved bug in project PORTAL created in the last 14 days, highest priority first.", a:"project = PORTAL AND issuetype = Bug AND resolution = Unresolved AND created >= -14d ORDER BY priority DESC"},
{q:"Your mock exam scores by CAPM domain: Fundamentals 78%, Predictive 72%, Agile 81%, Business Analysis 58%. Estimate the weighted score and say where the next two weeks go.", a:"0.36×78 + 0.17×72 + 0.20×81 + 0.27×58 = 28.1 + 12.2 + 16.2 + 15.7 ≈ 72%. Spend most of the time on Business Analysis (requirements, stakeholders, roadmaps), the biggest weighted gap."},
{q:"Classify each as project, program or portfolio decision: (a) choose which three cities to build next year; (b) coordinate twelve serving areas, a portal and a launch campaign for one city; (c) build serving area 7.", a:"(a) Portfolio. (b) Program. (c) Project."},
{q:"Design five dashboard tiles for the Oak Hollow sponsor, with a threshold for each.", a:"Example: SPI (green ≥ 0.95, red < 0.85); CPI (same thresholds); footage installed vs planned S-curve (red if more than 10% behind); top risks by EMV (red if any exceeds remaining contingency); safety, near misses reported this week (red if zero, because silence usually means under-reporting)."},
{q:"You have a high-school diploma and two years as a crew lead running look-aheads and production reports. What is the fastest credible path to the PMP?", a:"Pass CAPM now (it satisfies the 35-hour education requirement), keep documenting project leadership, move into coordinator or assistant-PM work, and apply once you have five years of qualifying experience within the last ten (high-school route); a degree shortens that to four or three years."}
],
checks:[{q:"CAPM format and entry requirement?",a:"150 questions (135 scored) in 180 minutes; secondary diploma plus 23 hours of PM education."},{q:"Current Project+ exam code and passing score?",a:"PK0-005; 710 on a 100–900 scale."},{q:"The three PMO types?",a:"Supportive, controlling, directive."}],
explain:"Present your 10-week CAPM plan to ADA as if ADA were your sponsor: the budget, the weekly hours, the domain weights you are targeting, and the evidence (mock scores by domain) that will tell you when to book."}
],
listening:[],
labs:[
{id:"p45lab1", title:"Charter and stakeholder grid for the Oak Hollow FTTH build", goal:"Initiate a fictional 412-home fiber build the way a PM would.", steps:["Write a half-page business case: 412 homes at about $1,200 each, a 40% take rate at $70 a month, simple payback, and one alternative you rejected.","Draft a one-page charter: SMART objectives, scope with explicit exclusions, five milestones, budget with reserves, top five risks, assumptions and constraints, success criteria, your authority, and a sponsor signature line.","List at least 12 stakeholders (ISP sponsor, city right-of-way office, pole owner, HOA, residents, 811, inspector, subcontractors...) and place each on a power-interest grid with current and desired engagement.","Write a RACI for six deliverables with exactly one A per row, and show it to ADA for critique."], deliverable:"Charter, business case, stakeholder grid and RACI as one PDF."},
{id:"p45lab2", title:"WBS and critical-path schedule", goal:"Build and analyze a real schedule in ProjectLibre or a spreadsheet.", steps:["Decompose Oak Hollow into a WBS to work-package level, obeying the 100% rule, with a short WBS dictionary entry for each package.","Give every activity a three-point estimate and compute PERT means and standard deviations.","Compute the forward and backward pass by hand for the seven-activity network in Module 2, then enter it in ProjectLibre (free, open source) or a spreadsheet and confirm the critical path and float match.","Crash the bore with a third rig and fast-track cable placement; record the new finish, the cost and the new critical path, then save a baseline."], deliverable:"WBS, network diagram, schedule screenshot with the critical path shown, and a one-paragraph crash-versus-fast-track recommendation."},
{id:"p45lab3", title:"Earned value worksheet", goal:"Turn as-built footage into earned value and a forecast.", steps:["Build a spreadsheet with ten weekly rows of planned footage, as-built footage and actual cost for the 24,000-ft bore at $10 a foot.","Add formula columns for PV, EV, AC, CV, SV, CPI, SPI, all three EACs, ETC, VAC and TCPI.","Chart PV, EV and AC as cumulative S-curves and mark the status date.","Write a five-line status report with red, amber or green set by thresholds you define before looking at the numbers."], deliverable:"The workbook, the chart and the status report."},
{id:"p45lab4", title:"Risk register with EMV", goal:"Quantify and price the risk on a fiber build.", steps:["Write 12 risks in cause–event–effect form, including at least one opportunity, one safety hazard taken from a job hazard analysis, and one cyber risk on the provisioning portal.","Score probability and impact on defined 1–5 scales, plot the matrix, and compute EMV for the top six.","Draw the bore-versus-aerial decision tree and size the contingency reserve from your EMVs.","Optional: run a 10,000-trial Monte Carlo of the Module 2 network in a spreadsheet or in Python (Domain 4) and read the P50 and P80 finish."], deliverable:"Register, matrix, decision tree, reserve calculation and (optionally) the P50/P80 dates."},
{id:"p45lab5", title:"Two-Sprint Scrum simulation on your own study backlog", goal:"Live Scrum by running it on your own learning in this app.", steps:["Set a Product Goal (for example: pass the CAPM by a chosen date) and build a Product Backlog from this app's remaining modules, labs and mock exams, sized in story points.","Write a Definition of Done for a module: lecture read, simple version, checks answered, Recall cards scheduled, a teach-back to ADA, and misses logged in the Mistake journal.","Run two one-week Sprints with a Sprint Goal each; keep a three-line Daily Scrum log, use the Focus timer for work blocks, and check Study analytics at the end of each Sprint.","Hold a Sprint Review (what is actually Done) and a retrospective with one owned, measured action; compute velocity and forecast the remaining Sprints as a range."], deliverable:"Backlog, two Sprint boards, a burndown, the velocity forecast and the retrospective actions."},
{id:"p45lab6", title:"Lessons-learned retrospective", goal:"Turn experience into an asset the next job can use.", steps:["Pick a real job segment you worked (or your Lab 5 Sprints) and build a timeline of what happened.","List what went well and what did not, in blameless language that describes conditions and decisions, not people.","Find root causes for the top two problems with five whys or a fishbone diagram.","Write three actions with owners and dates, and rewrite one checklist or job hazard analysis to include what you learned."], deliverable:"A lessons-learned entry and the updated checklist or JHA."},
{id:"p45lab7", title:"Mock CAPM question set", goal:"Test yourself the way PMI will.", steps:["Write 20 CAPM-style questions in proportion to the content outline (7 Fundamentals, 4 Predictive, 4 Agile, 5 Business Analysis), each with four options and an explanation.","Take this domain's exam and the cert-prep questions, then a full-length practice test from a reputable provider, under time at 1.2 minutes a question.","Score by domain, log every miss in the Mistake journal, and drill the misses with Mixed drill.","Write a two-week plan targeting your weakest weighted domain."], deliverable:"Your question set, scores by domain and the two-week plan."}
],
capstone:{title:"Run the job: a complete plan-and-control package for a hybrid FTTH launch", brief:"Plan and control a fictional ISP launch that combines a 412-home fiber build (predictive) with its provisioning portal (agile). Deliver a charter and stakeholder plan; a WBS and a critical-path schedule with a baseline; a budget with contingency and management reserves; a risk register with EMV, a Monte Carlo P80 date and a job hazard analysis for the riskiest field task; an earned-value status at a simulated midpoint with a forecast and a one-page status report; a two-Sprint plan for the portal synchronized to the 'first area lit' milestone; and a lessons-learned entry. Present it to ADA as you would to a sponsor.", rubric:["Charter, scope and WBS are complete (100% rule) with measurable objectives and named authority.","Schedule shows a correct forward and backward pass, float and critical path, plus a P80 date from Monte Carlo.","Earned value is computed correctly and the reported EAC is justified by the cause of the variance.","Risks are quantified (EMV), reserves are sized from them, and safety and cyber risks are included.","The hybrid plan synchronizes construction and Sprints at milestones, and the status report fits one page with decisions requested."]},
exam:[
{id:"p45q1", type:"mc", q:"Which is a project rather than operations?", options:["Answering fiber-cut trouble tickets","Quarterly OTDR audits of the live network","Building fiber to a new 412-home subdivision","Daily NOC monitoring"], answer:2, why:"A project is temporary and creates a unique result; the others are ongoing, repetitive operations."},
{id:"p45q2", type:"mc", q:"Which document formally authorizes a project and gives the PM authority to apply resources?", options:["Business case","Project charter","Scope statement","Communications plan"], answer:1, why:"The charter authorizes the project; the business case justifies it."},
{id:"p45q3", type:"mc", q:"An activity has O = 6, M = 9, P = 18 days. Its PERT estimate is:", options:["9.0 days","10.0 days","11.0 days","12.0 days"], answer:1, why:"(6 + 36 + 18) ÷ 6 = 60 ÷ 6 = 10.0 days."},
{id:"p45q4", type:"mc", q:"An activity has ES 10, EF 16, LS 24, LF 30. Its total float is:", options:["0 days","6 days","14 days","20 days"], answer:2, why:"Float = LS − ES = 24 − 10 = 14 (or LF − EF = 30 − 16)."},
{id:"p45q5", type:"mc", q:"EV = $102,000, AC = $112,000, PV = $120,000. Which is true?", options:["Ahead of schedule and under budget","Behind schedule and over budget","Behind schedule and under budget","Ahead of schedule and over budget"], answer:1, why:"SPI = 0.85 (behind) and CPI = 0.91 (over cost)."},
{id:"p45q6", type:"mc", q:"BAC = $240,000 and CPI = 0.80. If current cost performance continues, EAC is:", options:["$192,000","$240,000","$288,000","$300,000"], answer:3, why:"EAC = BAC ÷ CPI = 240,000 ÷ 0.80 = $300,000."},
{id:"p45q7", type:"mc", q:"A risk has a 25% probability and a $40,000 impact. Its EMV is:", options:["−$4,000","−$10,000","−$30,000","−$40,000"], answer:1, why:"0.25 × −40,000 = −$10,000."},
{id:"p45q8", type:"mc", q:"Buying insurance or subcontracting a risky crossing at a fixed price is which threat response?", options:["Avoid","Mitigate","Transfer","Accept"], answer:2, why:"Transfer shifts the impact and its ownership to a third party."},
{id:"p45q9", type:"mc", q:"Contingency reserve differs from management reserve because contingency:", options:["Covers unknown unknowns and sits outside the baseline","Covers identified risks and sits inside the cost baseline","Is controlled only by the sponsor","Is hidden padding in activity estimates"], answer:1, why:"Contingency is for known risks within the baseline; management reserve covers unknown unknowns outside it."},
{id:"p45q10", type:"mc", q:"Which contract type is typical for fiber construction paid per foot bored and per splice?", options:["Firm fixed price","Cost plus fixed fee","Unit price","Time and materials"], answer:2, why:"Unit-price contracts fix the rate and let quantities vary; the as-built quantities become the invoice."},
{id:"p45q11", type:"mc", q:"A team of 10 grows to 12. How many communication channels are added?", options:["2","11","21","66"], answer:2, why:"10 × 9 ÷ 2 = 45; 12 × 11 ÷ 2 = 66; 66 − 45 = 21."},
{id:"p45q12", type:"mc", q:"In the 2020 Scrum Guide, the commitment for the Increment is:", options:["The Product Goal","The Sprint Goal","The Definition of Done","The velocity target"], answer:2, why:"Product Backlog → Product Goal, Sprint Backlog → Sprint Goal, Increment → Definition of Done."},
{id:"p45q13", type:"mc", q:"A board holds 20 items in progress and finishes 5 per day. By Little's law the average cycle time is:", options:["0.25 days","4 days","15 days","100 days"], answer:1, why:"Cycle time = WIP ÷ throughput = 20 ÷ 5 = 4 days."},
{id:"p45q14", type:"mc", q:"An experienced, confident veteran is given a routine bore. The best situational-leadership style is:", options:["Directing","Coaching","Supporting","Delegating"], answer:3, why:"High competence and high commitment call for delegating the outcome."},
{id:"p45q15", type:"mc", q:"A crew member removes a guard from a saw. Which conflict mode fits?", options:["Withdraw/avoid","Smooth/accommodate","Force/direct","Compromise/reconcile"], answer:2, why:"Safety is non-negotiable: stop the work now (force/direct), then discuss."},
{id:"p45q16", type:"mc", q:"Which CAPM content-outline domain carries the second-largest weight?", options:["Predictive, Plan-Based Methodologies (17%)","Agile Frameworks/Methodologies (20%)","Business Analysis Frameworks (27%)","Fundamentals and Core Concepts (36%)"], answer:2, why:"36% Fundamentals, 27% Business Analysis, 20% Agile, 17% Predictive; candidates often under-study business analysis."},
{id:"p45q17", type:"sa", q:"At day 10 of a 20-day, $240,000 bore, PV = $120,000, EV = $102,000 and AC = $112,000. Compute CPI, SPI, an EAC, and TCPI, and write the one-sentence status you would send the sponsor.", answer:"CPI = 0.91, SPI = 0.85; EAC ≈ $263,500 if efficiency continues (or $250,000 if the overrun was a one-time event); TCPI = 138,000 ÷ 128,000 ≈ 1.08. Status: 'Bore amber, 15% behind and 9% over cost; forecast about $263,500 unless the rock issue is resolved; change order submitted; decision needed on test-boring the next segment.'", rubric:["Correct CPI and SPI","EAC computed with a stated assumption","TCPI correct and interpreted","Status gives numbers, cause, action and a decision request"]},
{id:"p45q18", type:"sa", q:"Explain why a 52-day plan built from most-likely durations is probably late, and how you would set a defensible commitment date.", answer:"Activity distributions are right-skewed and merge points multiply on-time probabilities (merge bias), so the most-likely sum is optimistic; a Monte Carlo of the Oak Hollow network made it only about 6% likely. Run a Monte Carlo (or use reference-class forecasting), commit externally to a percentile such as P80 (about 62 days), plan the crews to the 52-day schedule, and hold the gap as visible schedule contingency.", rubric:["Skew and merge bias explained","Monte Carlo or reference class named","Commitment set at a percentile, with the gap held as visible contingency"]}
],
cards:[
{id:"p45c1", front:"Project vs operations", back:"A project is temporary and creates a unique result (build the FTTH network); operations are ongoing and repetitive (run and repair it)."},
{id:"p45c2", front:"Triple constraint", back:"Scope, schedule and cost (quality in the middle): change one and another moves. Value, not the triangle, is the real target."},
{id:"p45c3", front:"Project charter", back:"The document that formally authorizes the project and gives the PM authority to apply resources; signed by the sponsor."},
{id:"p45c4", front:"Power-interest grid", back:"Stakeholder map: high/high manage closely, high power/low interest keep satisfied, low power/high interest keep informed, low/low monitor."},
{id:"p45c5", front:"Work breakdown structure", back:"Deliverable-oriented decomposition of the total scope into work packages; obeys the 100% rule and has a WBS dictionary."},
{id:"p45c6", front:"Critical path", back:"The longest path through the schedule network, the zero-float chain; any slip on it moves the finish date."},
{id:"p45c7", front:"Three-point estimate", back:"Optimistic, most likely, pessimistic. PERT mean = (O + 4M + P) ÷ 6, σ = (P − O) ÷ 6; triangular mean = (O + M + P) ÷ 3."},
{id:"p45c8", front:"Earned value", back:"Budgeted cost of the work actually performed (EV), compared with planned value (PV) and actual cost (AC) to measure progress in money."},
{id:"p45c9", front:"CPI / SPI", back:"CPI = EV ÷ AC (cost efficiency); SPI = EV ÷ PV (schedule efficiency). Below 1.0 is bad; EAC = BAC ÷ CPI if performance continues."},
{id:"p45c10", front:"Change control", back:"One path for every change: request, impact analysis, decision by the named authority (CCB), baseline update, communication."},
{id:"p45c11", front:"Risk register", back:"Living table of risks: cause–event–effect statement, probability, impact, score, owner, response, trigger, status."},
{id:"p45c12", front:"Expected monetary value", back:"EMV = probability × impact summed over outcomes (threats negative, opportunities positive); sizes contingency and drives decision trees."},
{id:"p45c13", front:"Contingency reserve", back:"Money or time for identified risks, inside the cost baseline, drawn down as risks occur; management reserve covers unknown unknowns outside it."},
{id:"p45c14", front:"Definition of Done", back:"The Increment's commitment in Scrum: the shared quality bar every item must meet to count as Done."},
{id:"p45c15", front:"Little's law", back:"Average cycle time = average WIP ÷ average throughput (stable system); cutting WIP cuts cycle time at the same throughput."},
{id:"p45c16", front:"Throughput", back:"In flow management, the number of work items finished per unit of time (closures a day, tickets a week)."},
{id:"p45c17", front:"Psychological safety", back:"A shared belief that the team is safe for interpersonal risk-taking (Edmondson, 1999): people report mistakes and near misses."},
{id:"p45c18", front:"Situational leadership", back:"Hersey and Blanchard: match style (directing, coaching, supporting, delegating) to each person's competence and commitment on the task."}
]
});
